[EncDescription_data/units/hmg/rus/7_62mm_SG_43]
7.62mm Medium Machine Gun M1943 (SG-43)

By the late 1930s, it had become clear that the older Maxim machine gun was quickly growing obsolete. The current alternative, the DS-39 machine gun, was not easily manufacturable and had reliability problems, so its mass production ended in July 1941. Work therefore began on a new air-cooled medium machine gun.
Fourteen different Soviet medium machine gun designs were drawn up between mid-1942 through early 1943. Eventually the field narrowed down to two prototypes: the DS-43 by Degtyaryov and the GVG by Goryunov. Comparative testing indicated that the GVG had lower projectile dispersion and higher reliability, and for these reasons it was adopted as the SG-43 on May 14, 1943. However, the mass production only began that October.
Compared with the Maxim, the SG-43 was lighter and more mobile, it had air-cooled barrel, and accepted both canvas and metal link belts. The SG-43 also was an adequate anti-aircraft gun, and it was both reliable and relatively easy to manufacture. At the same time, its main counterpart, the German MG 42, was clearly a more innovative design, and inspired the Soviets to continue developing new machine guns. Thus the service history of SG-43 was not as long as that of its predecessor.
SG-43 was actively used in the Great Patriotic War, after which the machine gun was redesignated the SGM, under which name it continued serving with the Soviet Army until it was finally replaced by the PK machine gun.
SG-43 machine guns were exported to over a dozen of countries, to North Korea in particular, and were actively use in the Korean War.

[EncDescription_data/units/hmg/rus/12_7mm_DShK]
12.7mm Heavy Machine Gun M1938 (DShK)

In 1929, the weapon designer V. Degtyaryov was entrusted with the creation of a new heavy machine gun. The prototype entered tests in 1930 and the DK model was adopted after a few improvements. Based on the DP-27 hand-held infantry machine gun, this new weapon used 30-round cartridge drums, which limited its rate of fire. The DK was produced in small quantities until 1935.
Eventually this machine gun was upgraded by G. Shpagin, who invented an improvement to the cartridge feed mechanism. In 1938, the new gun passed field tests, and was adopted in 1939, at which time it was designated the DShK (Degtyaryova-Shpagina Krupnokaliberny). Mass production was launched in 1940.
The DShK was a gas-operated, belt-fed, air-cooled machine gun with a rate of fire of 600 rounds per minute. It had a muzzle brake to reduce recoil, and was mounted on the Kolesnikov’s universal carrier, which allowed firing at both ground and airborne targets. The carrier featured wheels and a crew shield, both of which were removed when carrier was converted into a tripod, and the gun assumed its anti-aircraft position. Its ammunition was armor-piercing, and sometimes incendiary as well, and included cartridges of higher armor-piercing ability featuring tungsten carbide cores.
The DShK proved to be a reliable and powerful weapon, but on the downside, it was quite heavy, had several carriage drawbacks, and required valuable time to switch from its anti-aircraft to traveling position. In 1946, the machine gun was improved and redesignated the DShKM.
DShK was primarily used as an anti-aircraft machine gun. After December of 1942, the DShK, carried by anti-aircraft machine gun companies, remained the only means of air defense in the divisions, as well as in the armored brigades. Unfortunately, mass production of DShK could not meet demands, so anti-aircraft machine gun companies often had too few or no DShK machine guns at all. In particular, a number of Guard units had no DShK guns during the Battle of Kursk, despite their urgent need. By comparison, anti-aircraft artillery regiments were generally provided numerous DShK guns. For example, in 1943, one such a regiment had sixteen 37mm 61-K air defense guns and another sixteen DShK machine guns.
The DShK was actively produced throughout the war, and indeed its newer variants remained in production into the 1970s, and are still in active used today. About eight thousand DShK machine guns were built during the war.
Many DShK machine guns were mounted on heavy tanks, SPGs, armored trains, and on ships and boats. During the postwar years, the DShK had a long service history with the Soviet Army, and its newer variant, the DShKM, is still in service in Russia. A large number DShK guns were exported to various regimes friendly to the USSR, where they saw use in various armed conflicts. During the Korean War, the DShK was actively used by communists, primarily in the role of anti-aircraft.

