[EncDescription_data/units/hmg/rus/7_62mm_Maxim]
7.62mm Maxim Machine Gun M1910/30 on the Sokolov Mount 

In 1883, the inventor Hiram Maxim created the world’s first machine gun. Russia began buying them in small quantities in 1887, and it was not until 1902 that mass production was launched at the Tula Arms Plant. The first prototypes were built in 1905, and upgrades were immediately introduced. In 1910, mass production of the new variant was launched. It featured Sokolov’s light wheel mount instead of the cumbersome gun carriage, and a number of changes to the machine gun itself; some bronze parts were replaced with steel ones, and so on. This variant of the Maxim machine gun survived the World War I and the Civil Wars.
Late in the 1920s, work began on another Maxim variant, the result of which was the M1910/30 machine gun, which used heavy-bullet projectiles, and featured a new gun sight as well as some other minor changes. Some machine guns were mounted onto the universal M1931 mount, which also provided for anti-aircraft fire. In 1941, the machine gun underwent its final upgrade and received a wider water container mouth and some other changes to improve ease of manufacture.
The Maxim was a short-recoil, water-cooled, canvas-belt-fed machine gun which provided a rate of fire of 600 rounds per minute (and a 250-300 rounds per minute usable rate of fire). It also had a detachable wheel mount and a gun shield. The Maxim's accuracy range was up to 2000 meters, especially with heavy-bullet projectiles.
Without a doubt, the Maxim machine gun was a unique weapon. Its design was so successful that the gun lived well into World War II, and underwent a number of improvements, which led to simultaneous construction of multiple variants in several countries (the Wehrmacht’s MG 08, for example). However, by the late 1930s, it had clearly become obsolete. The main drawbacks were its heavy weight, the need for water cooling, the canvas belt feed, and the crew training required for proper assembly and maintenance of the unit. A new machine gun, the DS-39, was adopted with the plan that it would replace the Maxim’s machine gun by 1939. However, it proved prohibitively difficult to manufacture, so its production was curtailed when the war began, while production of the Maxim machine gun resumed. Nevertheless, development of a new machine gun continued, which eventually resulted in the creation of the SG-43 Goryunov machine gun, which entered mass production late in 1943. The Maxim machine gun was still produced in small quantities through 1945.
Until July of 1941, every company had a heavy machine gun squad carrying two guns. At that time, those guns were removed from the companies, only to reappear in December 1942 with the formation of single-gun MG squads. In addition, each battalion had a machine gun company (carrying nine to twelve MGs, depending on the personnel count). The Maxim machine gun served through the end of World War II, having become a symbol of the Soviet Army. Later it served in the Korean War as well, on the side of North Korea.

