[EncDescription_data/units/hmg/ger/7_92mm_MG_34]
7.92mm Maschinengewehr 34 General Purpose Machine Gun on Model 34 Tripod

By early 1930s, Germany decided to develop a general purpose machine gun, a portable weapon to be used as a light MG, medium MG, and to be mounted in vehicles. Such a design offered many advantages when completed, but designing it would prove to be difficult. Several companies answered the challenge, including Mauser and Rheinmetall AG. After a trial program in 1932, Rheinmetall was ordered to create a design combining the successful elements from several prototypes. The MG34 was the result, which was quickly accepted for service with the Wehrmacht. It went into mass production in 1936, after a series of minor changes.

The MG34 was a recoil-operated selective fire weapon with a short-recoiling barrel. Its rate of fire was 700-900 rounds per minute; when used as a medium machine gun, sustained rate of fire was about 300-350 rounds per minute. The fact that the weapon was air-cooled, combined with its high rate of fire, led to quick overheating. When firing in quick bursts, the barrel would need to be changed only after 250 rounds fired; changing barrels however took only about 7 seconds. The barrels had an overall lifespan of 6,000 rounds. 

Initially, the weapon was designed to be magazine-fed, using a double-drum 75-round mag; however in practice it was mostly belt-fed. The effective range of the MG34 was up to 1,500 meters.

In the medium machine gun role, the MG34 was set up on a universal Laffette 34 tripod. The stock bipod was not removed for this, making it easy to convert the weapon back into a light MG as needed. The MG34 tripod had a recoil-damping mechanism, increasing accuracy, could accommodate an optical sight, and allowed fire from prone and sitting positions; it could also be equipped with a special tripod, allowing use in an anti-aircraft role. The tripod was easily transported and set up. Its main drawback was the complicated design, making the tripod difficult to manufacture. Also, a special twin MG34 flak mount was available.

The MG34 was, without a doubt, a revolutionary weapon. German gun makers were the first to create a general purpose machine gun, capable of performing in different roles. Its high rate of fire, low weight, multiple feed types, ease of use, and reliability contributed to making it the most advanced machine gun of the period. However, the weapon was not without its drawbacks. The MG34 was expensive and required highly skilled labor, making it ill suited for mass production. The firing mechanism was also poorly suited for extreme conditions on the Eastern front, often leading to stoppages. These factors eventually led to it being supplanted by the MG42 machine gun.

Organizationally, the MG34 in its medium MG role was used in a machine-gun squad of an infantry battalion, consisting of 12 machine guns. The MG34 was used by German armed forces throughout the entire war, although by 1944-1945 it was being replaced by the MG42. Large-scale manufacture of the MG34 officially ended in 1942; however small production runs continued until the end of the war. A total of 475,988 MG34s were produced between 1936 and 1945.


[EncDescription_data/units/hmg/ger/7_92mm_MG_42]
7.92mm Maschinengewehr 42 General Purpose Machine Gun On Model 42 Tripod

The German High Command quickly realized that, despite all its advantages, the MG34 machine gun was poorly suited for mass production. As early as 1935, the work began on its replacement, and by 1937 a competition was launched to create a successor. Prototypes were tested in 1938, and a clear winner was the design by Dr. Werner Gruner of Grossfuss AG. Nevertheless, several shortcomings were also identified, and it took a lot of time addressing them. By late 1941, the first run of 1,500 machine guns were finally sent to the front-line units to be tested in the field. These led to another series of changes, and the resulting machine gun was finally accepted for service in early 1942.

The MG42 was a general purpose machine gun, a portable weapon that could be used as a light MG, medium MG, or be mounted in vehicles. Technically, the MG42 was a recoil-operated selective fire weapon with a short-recoiling barrel. The rate of fire could reach 1,200-1,300 rounds per minute. The barrel was air-cooled, which, combined with its high rate of fire, led to quick overheating. When firing in quick bursts, the barrel would need to be changed after about 250-300 rounds fired; changing barrels however took only about 5-8 seconds. The barrels had an overall lifespan of 4,000 rounds. The machine gun was belt-fed.

When used as a medium machine gun, the MG42 was installed on a universal Laffette 42 tripod. The stock bipod was not removed for this, making it easy to convert the weapon back into a light MG as needed. The MG42 tripod was very easy to use, and with an optical sight the effective range was up to 2,200 meters. With a special flak tripod, the weapon could be used in an anti-aircraft role. However, the tripod was less stable than the Laffette 34, which led to the development of a new Laffette 43, but it never went into mass production.

The MG42 is deservingly considered the best machine gun of the Second World War. Its main advantage was its unparalleled rate of fire (for which it was nicknamed Bonesaw), reliability, and durability even in adverse conditions. Due to the use of stamped and pressed steel parts, the weapon was easy to mass-produce.  Compared to the MG34, it was 30% cheaper and used 50% less steel. The few drawbacks of the weapons were mostly connected with its use in the light machine-gun role. Its rate of fire was excessive, and its large weight and size led to poor accuracy when using the stock bipod. 

Organizationally, the MG42 in its medium MG role would be used in a machine-gun squad of an infantry battalion, consisting of 12 machine-guns. The MG42 was used by German armed forces throughout the war on all fronts. Over 400,000 MG42s were produced between 1942 and 1945.

Modernized variants of the MG42 remain in production, and continue to be used by armed forces of several states to this day.

[EncDescription_data/units/hmg/usa/7_62mm_Browning_M1919A4]
Browning Machine Gun, .30 Cal., M1919A4

In 1917, the US Army adopted the Browning M1917 water-cooled heavy machine gun, which was more than sufficient for the time period. However, water cooling was not very convenient for aircraft and tanks, so an air-cooled variant was created in 1919.
The newer variant attracted the attention of the cavalry, which was looking for something lighter than the standard M1917. As a result, the cavalry units adopted the M1919A2, mounted on a light tripod based on the tank model. Later improvements brought about the M1919A3 and M1919A4 variants. The latter was adopted in 1934as a tank, anti-aircraft and mounted machine gun in.
Overall, the M1919A4 was a success. It was light, mobile and reliable. Of course, it was surpassed by the German MG 34 and MG 42, but it outshined the Maxim guns of the British and Soviets.
The M1919A4 was actively used by the US Army during World War II, and gradually replaced the M1917. The USSR also received a number of M1919A4s along with lend-leased tanks. The machine gun remained with the US Army until the 1960s, and was actively used in the Korean War. A large number of these machine guns were exported to US allies and served in a range of armed conflicts. In some regions the machine gun is still in use.
In 1942-43, the M1919A4 became the basis for the M1919A6 light machine gun. However, that model was too cumbersome and heavy for that class, so it was often used as a mounted MG.

[EncDescription_data/units/hmg/usa/12_7mm_Browning_M2]
Browning Machine Gun, .50 Cal., M2, HB, Flexible

At the end of the World War I, US military command required that the Army be equipped with large-caliber machine guns similar to those adopted by the Germans and French. To meet this task, the Winchester Repeating Arms Company developed a new Cal .50 (12.7mm) projectile, while the Browning Arms Company redesigned its M1917 machine gun to take that type of ammunition. Designated the M1921, the new water-cooled machine gun was adopted after a number of improvements, and later redesignated the M2.
Browning large-caliber machine guns were used by the US Army and Navy, and the Air Force was interested in them as well. However, water cooling was not acceptable for an aircraft machine gun (and the US Army was not much pleased with this limitation either). As a result, a new variant, the M2HB (Heavy Barrel) was created, and entered mass production in 1933. Its heavier and longer barrel avoided the need for water-cooling, and so the new model could be used on aircraft, tanks, and anti-aircraft self-propelled artillery, as well as mounted.

The М2НВ was quite a success. It was powerful, reliable, and light. Many feel it was the best large-caliber machine gun of World War II, and it certainly received very widespread use. These machine guns were the only standard air defense means of the US Army rifle divisions. These guns were also mounted on armored vehicles and various SPGs. The М2HB served throughout World War II is still used in various regional armed conflicts. The machine gun was primarily used against airborne targets, lightly armored vehicles, and gun emplacements. Along with tanks and SPGs, the USSR also received М2HB machine guns – the stationary, twin-gun variant – as a part of the lend-lease program.

[EncDescription_data/units/hmg/uk/7_7mm_Vickers_Mk_I]
.303 (7.7mm) Vickers Machine Gun Mark I on Mk.4 Tripod

The British Army accepted its first production run of the .303 Vickers machine gun in 1912. As many other machine guns of the era, the weapon was based on the successful Maxim Gun designed by Sir Hiram Maxim in 1884. The Vickers Limited company was one of the main manufacturers of the Maxim machine guns at the time; their machine guns were exported to Russia before independent production was set up there. After the Maxim manufacturing license ran out, Vickers created its own machine gun based on the proven design.

The Vickers machine gun with a light tripod was widely used by the British Army in the Great War, proving durable and extremely reliable. Many modifications were made during the course of the war. For instance, a special mount was designed which allowed the weapon to be used in an anti-aircraft role; and an air-cooled aviation variant was created to be mounted on aircraft. Variants of the weapon were also produced in the US, Australia and Portugal.

During the interwar period the British military leadership decided to phase Vickers and other medium machine guns out of service with infantry regiments, instead replacing them with the Bren light machine gun, and weapons installed on accompanying armoured vehicles. However after the start of the Second World War this tactic proved ill-advised, and the Vickers was returned to front-line service. By this time the design was rather obsolescent, but the British had no replacement. The new BESA machine gun was needed for the tanks, and besides it used a non-standard ammunition type. As result, the Vickers machine gun marched alongside the British Army in two world wars, and remained in use until the 1960s.